# Git Commit Conventions (twork-builder)

Adapted from awesome `git-conventional-commit-messages`. **This repo deviates from the plain spec — match the repo, not the spec.**

## Actual format in this repo

```
<type>: <DDMMYYYY> - <what changed>
```

Real examples from `git log`:

```
feat: 13072026 - add Python sync pipeline and hand-written block suite
fix: 13072026 - refine ambulance process section editor controls and styling
chore: 13072026 - bump plugin to v1.0.8 with synchronized block sources
docs: 13072026 - remove author email from README for privacy
```

- **MUST** keep the `type: DDMMYYYY - description` shape — no parenthesised scope, date before the description
- **MUST** use the date of the commit, `DDMMYYYY`, zero-padded
- **MUST** write the description in lower case, imperative-ish, no trailing period
- **MUST** name the concrete surface in the description (block name, include file, script) — the scope lives in the words, not in parentheses
- Run `git log --oneline -5` before writing a message and match what you see. If the repo convention has moved on, follow the newest commits, not this file.

## Types in use
`feat` · `fix` · `chore` · `docs` — plus `refactor`, `perf`, `build`, `style` when they genuinely apply. Do not invent new types.

## Breaking changes
Block markup or attribute changes that invalidate existing posts are breaking for site editors even when PHP still runs.
- Append `!` after the type: `feat!: 25082026 - ...`
- Or add a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer naming the affected `twork/*` blocks and whether a `deprecated` entry ships with it

## Body / footers (optional)
- Blank line after the subject; body explains **why**, not a file list — the diff is the file list
- Footers in git-trailer form (`Refs:`, `BREAKING CHANGE:`)

## Never
- `wip`, `fix`, `update`, `changes` as the whole message
- A subject that only restates the filename (`fix: 25082026 - update edit.js`)
- Bundling a version bump, a build artifact, and a feature into one commit — split them
- Committing `build/`, `dist/`, `node_modules/`, or a release zip outside a release commit, and never silently
- Rewriting published history

## Branches
- `type/short-description` (Clickr standard), e.g. `fix/packages-section-prep-panel`
- No Gitflow `develop` / `release/*` / `hotfix/*` layer here — this repo works off `main`
- Never commit directly to `main` for feature work; branch first

## Release commits
A version bump touches four places and they move in one commit: `twork-builder.php` header, `TWORK_BUILDER_VERSION`, `readme.txt` stable tag, `package.json`.
